//Operator Overloading

Operator overloading means giving an operator (+, -, ==, <, etc.) a special meaning for objects of your class.

For example, normally:

int a = 10, b = 20;
int c = a + b;

+ already knows how to add integers.

But suppose we have:

class Point {
public:
    int x, y;
};

We may want:

Point p1, p2, p3;
p3 = p1 + p2;

C++ doesn't automatically know how + should work for Point objects.

We can overload +:

class Point {
public:
    int x, y;

    Point operator+(Point p) {
        Point temp;
        temp.x = x + p.x;
        temp.y = y + p.y;
        return temp;
    }
};

Now:

p3 = p1 + p2;

can work according to the behavior we defined.

Simple definition

Operator overloading = defining how an existing operator behaves when used with objects of a class.

It is also an example of compile-time polymorphism.